<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c3ab04bf-910a-4162-9afa-620287c67f5c> <https://schema.org/description> "The Biological Museum in Stockholm was opened in 1893 on the initiative of conservator Gustaf Kolthoff. Right from the start, several hundred preserved birds and other animals were exhibited in a diorama with background paintings by the Swedish artist Bruno Liljefors. Since 1970 the Biological Museum has been part of the Skansen Foundation/Stiftelsen Skansen." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c3ab04bf-910a-4162-9afa-620287c67f5c> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(18.09769 59.32712)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c3ab04bf-910a-4162-9afa-620287c67f5c> <https://schema.org/logo> <https://www.skansen.se/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/logo.svg> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/69095868-3ddd-4b79-a005-ed3bc12800bb> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/69095868-3ddd-4b79-a005-ed3bc12800bb> <https://schema.org/name> "National Genebank of Tunisia (NGBT)" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/69095868-3ddd-4b79-a005-ed3bc12800bb> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NGBT" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/69095868-3ddd-4b79-a005-ed3bc12800bb> <https://schema.org/description> "The National Genebank of Tunisia (NGBT) is an administrative institution under the authority of the Ministry of Local Affairs and Environment of Tunisia. NGBT started its activities in November 2007 with the mandate to coordinate and promote the conservation and sustainable use of plants, animals and micro-organisms. NGBT has a capacity for ex situ conservation, cryopreservation and has a 20 hectare field genebank in Takelsa (Governorate of Nabeul, north-eastern Tunisia). Currently, the seed genebank conserves about 40,000 accessions, including a large diversity of crop species such as cereal crops that have been historically staple crops in North Africa." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/69095868-3ddd-4b79-a005-ed3bc12800bb> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(10.211884 36.84202)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5c41962-8860-4019-920f-32651ee724db> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5c41962-8860-4019-920f-32651ee724db> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5c41962-8860-4019-920f-32651ee724db> <https://schema.org/name> "Centro visita del Parco nazionale d'Abruzzo Lazio e Molise di Ortona dei Marsi - Entoma" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5c41962-8860-4019-920f-32651ee724db> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "PNALM-ODM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5c41962-8860-4019-920f-32651ee724db> <https://schema.org/description> "Insect Documentation Centre, established thanks to a collaboration with the Municipal Council and Prof. Enrico Migliaccio, a highly experienced and passionate entomologist. Some of the Park’s historic entomological collections are housed in the “Amalia Taglieri” room. Around 400 boxes, including the historic collection of P. Luigioni, which stands out for its extensive range of beetles, and the collections of C. Prola and V. & S. Olivieri, which have enriched the knowledge of the Park’s butterflies and moths, both diurnal and nocturnal. Entoma also hosts a permanent exhibition of extraordinary photographs of insects, taken by the photographer Angelina Iannarelli. These images, displayed along the route leading to the collections hall, are arranged according to systematic criteria, from the most primitive orders to the most evolved insects. Some of these photographs were donated by Roberto Antonelli, and the identification work was supported by renowned entomologists such as Romolo Fochetti, Paolo Fontana, Maurizio Pavesi, Marino Quaranta and Marco Valle." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/9be1bf66-64df-4c1b-97af-dce6bc8055fc> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/9be1bf66-64df-4c1b-97af-dce6bc8055fc> <https://schema.org/name> "Somali Region Livestock and Agricultural Research Institute" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/9be1bf66-64df-4c1b-97af-dce6bc8055fc> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "SRLAR" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/9be1bf66-64df-4c1b-97af-dce6bc8055fc> <https://schema.org/description> "Hosted at the SoRLARI head office in Jigjiga, capital of the Somali Region, the herbarium serves as an essential reference resource for researchers, students, and conservation practitioners. It currently houses a rich collection of the region’s indigenous flora, comprising some 5,000 plant specimens that span 127 families, 603 genera and over 1,500 individual species." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/9be1bf66-64df-4c1b-97af-dce6bc8055fc> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(42.822528 9.353585)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

