<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b1a5b244-b055-4178-ae4f-36fbaeb31620> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b1a5b244-b055-4178-ae4f-36fbaeb31620> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b1a5b244-b055-4178-ae4f-36fbaeb31620> <https://schema.org/name> "Ţării Crişurilor Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b1a5b244-b055-4178-ae4f-36fbaeb31620> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "TCM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b1a5b244-b055-4178-ae4f-36fbaeb31620> <https://schema.org/description> "Ţării Crişurilor Museum – museum complex, subordinated to Bihor County Council – is an important cultural objective in Romania, formed by Ţării Crişurilor Museum departments: Archaeology, History, Natural Sciences, Ethnography, Art, Public Relations and Exhibitions), The “Iosif Vulcan” Memorial Museum, the “Aurel Lazăr” Memorial Museum, the “Ady Endre” Memorial Museum (museums that are part of the Memorial museums, social groups, history of the minorities) and the Vadu Crișului Cave. The Department of Natural Sciences was founded in 1971. Its fundamental objective was the preservation, research and development of the collections of geology, paleontology and biology, owned by the History Department since 1952. The basis of these collections was laid by Tiberiu Jurcsák and his collaborators in 1950 and then enriched by the scientific staff of the department. The patrimony of the department has increased to a number of 158.385 registered pieces, of which over 84.000 in the field of geology-paleontology and over 48.000 in the field of biology. The collections are structured on two domains: paleontology and geological evolution of the western part of Romania, with the Triassic marine vertebrates, fossil plants from the Inferior Jurassic, dinosaurs from the Cretaceous and Pleistocene vertebrates; and biology, botany and zoology, with remarkable malacological and oological collections." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b1a5b244-b055-4178-ae4f-36fbaeb31620> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(21.92382 47.050933)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b1a5b244-b055-4178-ae4f-36fbaeb31620> <https://schema.org/logo> <https://mtariicrisurilor.ro/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Logo-Muzeu-Alb-nou.png> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/204441ae-d619-45fb-b38f-4eb2cda1e360> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/204441ae-d619-45fb-b38f-4eb2cda1e360> <https://schema.org/name> "Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Forestry Research and Training" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/204441ae-d619-45fb-b38f-4eb2cda1e360> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "UWIFORT" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/204441ae-d619-45fb-b38f-4eb2cda1e360> <https://schema.org/description> "The Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Forestry Research and Training (formerly known as the Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Conservation and Environment, UWICER) was established in 2004 at Lamai Goempa in Bumthang with support from the MacArthur Foundation and the Bhutan Trust Fund for Environmental Conservation. Its original purpose was to provide high-quality training and education in forestry and environmental studies, taking over forestry training programs such as the one-year Certificate in Forestry. Over time the institute underwent several reorganizations and name changes based on government directives. In 2009, it was renamed to emphasize conservation and environmental research with goals to contribute to scientific knowledge, influence environmental policy, and train conservation leaders. In 2017, it became the Ugyen Wangchuck Institute for Conservation and Environment Research (UWICER) with an explicit mandate to conduct forestry and environmental research in addition to training professionals. Later reorganizations led to additional structural changes, and in 2023 it was renamed to its current title, reinforcing its role in forestry research and training. Today, the institute focuses on rigorous science-based research, capacity building, and transferring knowledge to practitioners, policymakers, and future leaders in conservation and environmental management. " .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/204441ae-d619-45fb-b38f-4eb2cda1e360> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(90.72484 27.545582)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/fd2e0e06-c555-45e0-a456-03c8861b1988> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/fd2e0e06-c555-45e0-a456-03c8861b1988> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/fd2e0e06-c555-45e0-a456-03c8861b1988> <https://schema.org/name> "Museo de la Fundación Oceánica - Coleccion Marina" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/fd2e0e06-c555-45e0-a456-03c8861b1988> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "FOCM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/fd2e0e06-c555-45e0-a456-03c8861b1988> <https://schema.org/description> "La colección de mamíferos marinos del Museo de Fauna Marina de la Fundación Oceánica Ecuador (FOCM) reúne actualmente 160 especímenes correspondientes a 16 especies, recolectados entre 2009 y 2026. Los especímenes provienen de varamientos, monitoreo de biodiversidad , donaciones y actividades de investigación científica a lo largo de la costa continental del Ecuador, con énfasis en la provincia de Manabí. La colección integra además materiales derivados de investigaciones desarrolladas en colaboración con Pacific Whale Foundation en Ecuador, así como recolecciones oportunistas realizadas durante operaciones en el mar por Palo Santo Travel (raspados, animales flotantes). Incluye material osteológico (cráneos, huesos y esqueletos parciales), parásitos y muestras biológicas preservadas (tejidos, piel y otros), principalmente de cetáceos y otros vertebrados marinos. Los registros están georreferenciados y documentados bajo estándares Darwin Core, lo que permite su integración en plataformas internacionales de acceso abierto como GBIF. La colección tiene como objetivo apoyar la investigación científica, fortalecer el conocimiento de la biodiversidad marina del Pacífico suroriental y contribuir a la conservación de especies y ecosistemas marinos, incluyendo áreas protegidas del Ecuador. **ENGLISH** The marine mammal collection of the Museo de Fauna Marina of Fundación Oceánica Ecuador (FOCM) currently comprises 160 specimens representing 16 species, collected between 2009 and 2025. Specimens originate from strandings, biodiversity monitoring, and scientific research conducted along the continental coast of Ecuador, with a focus on the Manabí region. The collection also incorporates materials derived from research conducted in collaboration with Pacific Whale Foundation in Ecuador, as well as opportunistic collections obtained during marine operations by Palo Santo Travel. The collection includes osteological material (skulls, bones, and partial skeletons) and preserved biological samples (tissues, skin, and others), primarily from cetaceans and other marine vertebrates. All records are georeferenced and curated following Darwin Core standards, enabling their integration into international open-access platforms such as GBIF. The collection aims to support scientific research, enhance the understanding of marine biodiversity in the Southeast Pacific, and contribute to the conservation of marine species and ecosystems, including protected areas in Ecuador." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/fd2e0e06-c555-45e0-a456-03c8861b1988> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-80.80539 -1.5555)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b39d6c2-063d-44a6-8b93-e48fa56f8467> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b39d6c2-063d-44a6-8b93-e48fa56f8467> <https://schema.org/name> "Zoo New England" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b39d6c2-063d-44a6-8b93-e48fa56f8467> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "ZOONEWENG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b39d6c2-063d-44a6-8b93-e48fa56f8467> <https://schema.org/description> "Zoo New England is a private, non-profit corporation that operates Franklin Park Zoo in Boston and Stone Zoo in Stoneham, Massachusetts. Franklin Park Zoo and Stone Zoo were founded in 1912 and 1905, respectively, and both are independently accredited by the Association of Zoos & Aquariums (AZA). Zoo New England's mission is to inspire people to protect and sustain the natural world for future generations by creating fun and engaging experiences that integrate wildlife and conservation programs, research, and education. Together, the two zoos are home to more than 300 species, with biological specimens archived opportunistically from routine healthcare examinations or necropsies. Thousands of archived biospecimens are paired with detailed health data, often spanning an animal’s lifetime." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b39d6c2-063d-44a6-8b93-e48fa56f8467> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-71.08978 42.305279)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .
