<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b00dedcd-dada-4ab8-b769-bc1258c49c55> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b00dedcd-dada-4ab8-b769-bc1258c49c55> <https://schema.org/name> "Museo ornitologico di San Gimignano" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b00dedcd-dada-4ab8-b769-bc1258c49c55> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "MOSG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b00dedcd-dada-4ab8-b769-bc1258c49c55> <https://schema.org/description> "The city's ancient 16th-century church, the former Church of San Francesco, houses the San Gimignano Ornithological Museum, which features a rich and varied collection of birds from various parts of Italy and Europe. Owned by Marchioness Marianna Panciatichi Ximenes d'Aragona Paolucci, a renowned Florentine malacologist and passionate ornithologist, the collection was a gift from the Marchioness to the Municipality of San Gimignano in 1927. The original collection numbered 1,260 specimens; today, the museum displays approximately 370 bird specimens, belonging to 253 different species, including rare or endangered ones, formerly kept on the estate of Villa del Monte, a historic building in the city. The exhibition, curated by the Department of Environmental Biology of the University of Siena and the National Institute of Game Biology, allows visitors to immerse themselves perfectly in the surrounding fauna." .

