<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c58479a9-aae7-45be-9812-a865a9243727> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c58479a9-aae7-45be-9812-a865a9243727> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c58479a9-aae7-45be-9812-a865a9243727> <https://schema.org/name> "Niigata University Forest" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c58479a9-aae7-45be-9812-a865a9243727> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NUF" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c58479a9-aae7-45be-9812-a865a9243727> <https://schema.org/description> "Niigata University Forest is located in Koda, Sado City, Niigata Prefecture. It encompasses approximately 500 hectares of forest on the western slope of the Ohsado Mountains in northern Sado Island. Established in 1955 through the transfer of prefectural forest land, it relocated its lodging and offices to the current site in 1992. In 2019, it was integrated with the Coastal Laboratory and other facilities to form the current Center. The forest, spreading across mountainous terrain at elevations of 270 to 947 meters, is primarily composed of natural cedar stands. It maintains a high degree of natural integrity, including diverse broadleaf trees characteristic of the beech zone and wetland vegetation. Groups of cedar trees exhibiting distinctive growth forms under snowy and windy conditions can also be observed. Today, it is positioned primarily as an educational and research site emphasizing coexistence with nature and biodiversity conservation rather than timber production. It is utilized for fieldwork and collaborative research by universities and research institutions nationwide. Diverse research themes are pursued, including forest dynamics, climate change responses, and island ecosystem studies. The center also participates in long-term observation projects such as the “Biodiversity Monitoring 1000” initiative. As a hub for learning about the coexistence of nature and people on Sado Island, it actively engages in regional collaboration." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c58479a9-aae7-45be-9812-a865a9243727> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(138.387024 38.230972)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/65c860d6-866b-4b79-94b6-1fe20cfde100> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/65c860d6-866b-4b79-94b6-1fe20cfde100> <https://schema.org/name> "Native Bee Society of BC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/65c860d6-866b-4b79-94b6-1fe20cfde100> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NBSBC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/65c860d6-866b-4b79-94b6-1fe20cfde100> <https://schema.org/description> "The Native Bee Society of BC is an incorporated not-for-profit society, established in July 2019 in the province of British Columbia under the Societies Act. As of May 2023, NBSBC is an affiliate club of BC Nature. We are a collective of native bee researchers, artists, land managers, conservation practitioners, and enthusiasts working to promote the conservation of native bees in British Columbia. " .
