<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f36ae891-c532-4415-a3b8-5576fd5aab46> <https://schema.org/description> "The ZOMB, a Korea National Research Resource Bank, was established with the support of Ministry of Science and Technology and National Research Foundation of Korea in 2003." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f444dc20-d121-4462-8ea0-2a53df1444ad> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f444dc20-d121-4462-8ea0-2a53df1444ad> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f444dc20-d121-4462-8ea0-2a53df1444ad> <https://schema.org/name> "Russian Academy of Sciences, Zoological Institute, Zoological Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f444dc20-d121-4462-8ea0-2a53df1444ad> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "ZIN" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f444dc20-d121-4462-8ea0-2a53df1444ad> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2025062> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72a7a19-5c28-4ee1-8368-d9f100ea3a38> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72a7a19-5c28-4ee1-8368-d9f100ea3a38> <https://schema.org/name> "National Zoological Gardens of South Africa" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72a7a19-5c28-4ee1-8368-d9f100ea3a38> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NZG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72a7a19-5c28-4ee1-8368-d9f100ea3a38> <https://schema.org/description> "The NZG Biobank acts as an integrated resource linking the multiple tissue collections (Tissue bank; DNA bank; Necropsy sample bank; Cell culture bank; Sperm bank) and provides samples and information to an interdisciplinary wildlife research and management audience regionally and globally. Sample providers include national and provincial parks, wildlife ranches and zoological institutions, while users include HEI&rsquo;s, research institutions, wildlife owners and others. Through the Biobank, a single port of entry for organisations requiring African wildlife biomaterials for research, biodiversity conservation and biotechnology development purposes has been established. An advisory committee oversees operational efficiency and strategy. Members represented on the committee are experts drawn from the stakeholder community, biomaterial sample providers, sample users, academics, national and provincial regulatory authorities. The NZG Biobank is housed in a dedicated building with offices, fully equipped laboratories for processing and cryo-preservation, storerooms, and multiple rooms with fridges, freezers ranging from 4 degrees C to minus 150 degrees C, to ultralow (liquid nitrogen) temperatures. The Bank coordinates, collects, processes and stores different tissue types such as extracted DNA, ear-notches, hair, feathers, semen, organs etc. and maintains the samples under different and optimal storage conditions that are suitable for future research and management applications." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72fd8bb-1d02-45f7-a686-b28d6529b4a2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72fd8bb-1d02-45f7-a686-b28d6529b4a2> <https://schema.org/name> "Rutgers University" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72fd8bb-1d02-45f7-a686-b28d6529b4a2> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "CHRB" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72fd8bb-1d02-45f7-a686-b28d6529b4a2> <https://schema.org/description> "The Chrysler Herbarium (CHRB) at Rutgers University is the largest internationally recognized and most important research herbarium in the state of New Jersey (USA). With nearly 200,000 botanical specimens of plants, algae, and fungi, it covers a vast range of botanical biodiversity. The collection consists of scientifically field collected, dried, and preserved specimens that are curated and cataloged taxonomically. New Jersey, especially endangered species and historical specimens dating from 1850; William L. Hutcheson Memorial Forest; New Jersey pinelands; Willowwood Arboretum. All groups of vascular plants, algae, mosses, lichens, liverworts, and fungi. The Rutgers Mycological Collection (RUTPP) includes many important pathogens." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72fd8bb-1d02-45f7-a686-b28d6529b4a2> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q7382826> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f72fd8bb-1d02-45f7-a686-b28d6529b4a2> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-74.437547 40.487825)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f7bfca4c-0a8a-463b-bd3d-8363e0f02aee> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f7bfca4c-0a8a-463b-bd3d-8363e0f02aee> <https://schema.org/name> "Romanian Bioresource Centre and Advanced Research Association" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f7bfca4c-0a8a-463b-bd3d-8363e0f02aee> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "RBCAR" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f7bfca4c-0a8a-463b-bd3d-8363e0f02aee> <https://schema.org/description> "the association is a professional NGO with the purpose to setup some standards of culture collection in Romania and organize a network of culture collections in Romania" .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f84541b4-c7aa-4d96-bd1f-a084847f23fd> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f84541b4-c7aa-4d96-bd1f-a084847f23fd> <https://schema.org/name> "Australian Tropical Herbarium" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f84541b4-c7aa-4d96-bd1f-a084847f23fd> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "CNS" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f84541b4-c7aa-4d96-bd1f-a084847f23fd> <https://schema.org/description> "The Australian Tropical Herbarium (ATH) is a joint venture of the Commonwealth Scientific and Industrial Research Organisation (CSIRO), Director National Parks (DNP), Queensland Department of Environment and Resource Management (DERM), Queensland Department of Employment, Economic Development and Innovation (DEEDI), and James Cook University (JCU). The ATH boasts state-of-the-art facilities and infrastructure for specimen processing and curation, photography, pest and climate control, and field, herbarium and laboratory research. The main activity is management of the more than 160,000 plant specimens that comprise the CNS collection, a merger of the collections (and staff) of the Australian National Herbarium Atherton (QRS), the Queensland Herbarium Mareeba (MBA) and part of the JCU Herbarium collection (JCT) to form an unsurpassed representative collection of north Queensland's flora. Research at the Australian Tropical Herbarium covers a range of topics including tropical plant and fungal taxonomy and evolution, ethnobotany, ecology, climate change studies, development of identification products, agroforestry and regional ecosystem mapping. We currently have two numbering systems: one for DNA and one for all other collections (herbarium sheets, spirit, woodblocks). For non DNA specimens- CNS:12345 ; For DNA materials- CNS(DNA):12345 This will change once we revise the management of DNA materials; these will be databased according to the voucher specimen held in our main collection. Australia's tropics, especially northeastern Australian rainforests; related plant groups of Papua New Guinea, Pacific, and southeast Asia." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f84541b4-c7aa-4d96-bd1f-a084847f23fd> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(145.687 -16.816)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

