<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4ae5493e-7a8d-4d3e-a657-9279174c7bf1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4ae5493e-7a8d-4d3e-a657-9279174c7bf1> <https://schema.org/name> "Center for Environmental Management of Military Lands Plant Collection" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4ae5493e-7a8d-4d3e-a657-9279174c7bf1> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "CEMML" .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b99a867-7929-47e8-90df-9bcd058d42e9> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b99a867-7929-47e8-90df-9bcd058d42e9> <https://schema.org/name> "Karlsruher Institute of Technology - Botanical Garden" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b99a867-7929-47e8-90df-9bcd058d42e9> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "KITBG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4b99a867-7929-47e8-90df-9bcd058d42e9> <https://schema.org/description> "The garden contains a total of 5,700 plant species, including 1,930 endangered species per the IUCN Red List or CITES conventions. Research focus: specific projects in Arabidopsis thaliana, Gnetum gnemon, Nicotiana tabacum, Oryza sativa, and Vitis vinifera. The garden currently cultivates over 50 species of wild grape vines for use in research against downy mildew, as well as a collection of wild rice species from all over the world." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4cf93dd2-06c8-4f49-89bc-a5d0805c6747> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4cf93dd2-06c8-4f49-89bc-a5d0805c6747> <https://schema.org/name> "ZE Botanischer Garten und Botanisches Museum, Freie Universität Berlin" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4cf93dd2-06c8-4f49-89bc-a5d0805c6747> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "B" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4cf93dd2-06c8-4f49-89bc-a5d0805c6747> <https://schema.org/description> "Updated Mar 2013 (staff updates). Specimens of B-W (Herbarium Willdenow) not sent on loan, but photographs provided. Herbarium destroyed by fire in 1943 except B-W, type material of selected angiosperms, pteridophytes, fungi p.p. (Uredineae and fungi imperfecti), and special collections (cf. Englera 7: 219-252. 1987; http://www.bgbm.fu-berlin.de/bgbm/research/colls/herb/). Special collections of fruits and seeds, gymnosperm cones, spirit-preserved material, wood, fossils, and plant remains from Egyptian tombs. All groups worldwide, especially central Europe, Mediterranean area, southwestern Asia, Africa, Cuba, and South America." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4cf93dd2-06c8-4f49-89bc-a5d0805c6747> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(13.305618 52.454738)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4f9c1825-bb91-444a-894d-aca6c604fe54> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4f9c1825-bb91-444a-894d-aca6c604fe54> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4f9c1825-bb91-444a-894d-aca6c604fe54> <https://schema.org/name> "Indiana State Museum and Historic Sites" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4f9c1825-bb91-444a-894d-aca6c604fe54> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "71" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/4f9c1825-bb91-444a-894d-aca6c604fe54> <https://schema.org/description> "The Natural History & Archaeology section assembles meaningful collections from which to interpret the history of life and environments, and cultures in Indiana. The museum has a strong program of field investigation and recovery. Collections are developed in three broad categories: Geology (strong in invertebrate fossils and late Pleistocene vertebrates), Biology, and Archaeology (with strong representation of select prehistoric and historic sites). There are some 500,000 items and lots in the collections." .
