<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f9c9b2d4-5c90-44fa-977a-7fd19451f6ad> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f9c9b2d4-5c90-44fa-977a-7fd19451f6ad> <https://schema.org/name> "Russell-Cotes Art Gallery & Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f9c9b2d4-5c90-44fa-977a-7fd19451f6ad> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "BORGM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f9c9b2d4-5c90-44fa-977a-7fd19451f6ad> <https://schema.org/description> "The RCAGM is a historic house (completed 1901) with an additional wing of public art galleries (1919-1926) and a modern extension (1991). Based around a core collection formed by our Founders Sir Merton (1835-1921 and Lady Russell-Cotes (1835-1920) which were used to decorate their home and their business, the Royal Bath Hotel, in Bournemouth. World travellers, our Founders collected wherever they went and as well as souvenirs, examples of local cultures and fine art they also purchased natural history items. Natural history seems to have been particular interest of Lady Russell-Cotes but she does not seem to have taken a scientific approach to the topic. On top of what they left us there is 100 years worth of other donations and acquisitions, which as regards natural science, tend towards the more systematically collected with biological data collected. The RCAGM has invested heavily in inventorying, cataloguing and digitising its collections but there is still much work to do with its natural history collections, particularly around identification and reconciliation against the paper records it holds. The RCAGM has not undertaken or participated in natural science research projects as a result. The natural science material it does display is for the purpose of interpreting the historic house part of the building to its original appearance of the home of wealthy period collectors. The majority of our natural history collection is held at our off-site store and at the Hampshire Cultural Trust facility in Winchester. In the long term we would like to retain only the 'exotic' cased specimens and transfer everything else, especially the more scientifically collected and preserved items such as study skins, to more appropriate public collections." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f9c9b2d4-5c90-44fa-977a-7fd19451f6ad> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-1.8708 50.7176)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

