<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/233164b5-2688-42d7-bdd1-89927310484a> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/233164b5-2688-42d7-bdd1-89927310484a> <https://schema.org/name> "Institute of Biology of the University of Latvia" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/233164b5-2688-42d7-bdd1-89927310484a> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "LUBI" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/233164b5-2688-42d7-bdd1-89927310484a> <https://schema.org/description> "The Institute of Biology of the University of Latvia (UL) was founded in 1951 as the Research Institute of the Latvian Academy of Sciences (director prof. Alfrēds Ozols (1952–1965)). During this period different scientific sectors and research directions were established and developed: botany, plant genetics, plant mineral feeding, plant physiology, bioindication, hydrobionite biochemistry, animal physiology, experimental entomology, marine and inland waters hydrobiology, ornithology, parasitology and radiobiology. In the following years (directors prof. A. Valdmanis (1965–1971) and prof. G. Andrušaitis (1972–1998)), some research directions such as parasitology, radiobiology and plant physiology were closed, but others were created: geobotany, hormone biochemistry, magnetobiology, genomics and bioinformatics. Following the restoration of Latvia's independence in 1991, the Latvian Academy of Sciences transformed into European-type academy and the Institute of Biology became a legally independent unit of the UL, joining UL as a basic entity in 2016. The Institute is the leading research centre in biology in Latvia. The institute currently comprises 12 laboratories." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/233164b5-2688-42d7-bdd1-89927310484a> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q39774585> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/233164b5-2688-42d7-bdd1-89927310484a> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(24.082608 56.931183)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/acd4e796-ba9b-4b62-857e-d96c2e1bca6e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/acd4e796-ba9b-4b62-857e-d96c2e1bca6e> <https://schema.org/name> "Istituto per la protezione sostenibile delle piante di Torino" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/acd4e796-ba9b-4b62-857e-d96c2e1bca6e> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "CNR-IPSP-TO" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/acd4e796-ba9b-4b62-857e-d96c2e1bca6e> <https://schema.org/description> "The Institute for Sustainable Protection of Plants (CNR-IPSP) is aimed at valorising plants of agricultural and forestry interest, strengthening natural antagonisms and biocontrol methods of plant parasites and weeds, improving the quality and quantity of agri-food production, selecting and remediating valuable plant germplasm, characterizing and producing bio-molecules of agro-industrial interest, mitigating the impacts of global change, promoting sustainable and environmentally friendly growth. Activities at CNR-IPSP are focused to integrate specific knowledge in the sectors of nematology, entomology, pathology, viral and virus-like phytopathogens, mycorrhizal fungi and bacteria, biostimulators and biofertilizers and defense from environmental stress. Worldwide. Plants, Bacteria, Fungi, Viruses, Animals (Nematodes)" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/acd4e796-ba9b-4b62-857e-d96c2e1bca6e> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(7.641918 45.017314)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .
