<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/57a6e7b4-9fde-4efb-be3b-b800a6dd72f5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/57a6e7b4-9fde-4efb-be3b-b800a6dd72f5> <https://schema.org/name> "Herbario Padre Luis Sodiro (QPLS)" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/57a6e7b4-9fde-4efb-be3b-b800a6dd72f5> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "QPLS" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/57a6e7b4-9fde-4efb-be3b-b800a6dd72f5> <https://schema.org/description> "El Herbario Padre Luis Sodiro (QPLS) es una colección histórica que contiene las muestras recolectadas por el padre Luis Sodiro, S.J. (1836-1909) así como de otros padres jesuitas. Este es parte del Centro Cultural Biblioteca Ecuatoriana Aurelio Espinosa Pólit. La colección de plantas del Herbario Padre Luis Sodiro (QPLS) contiene muestras recolectadas entre 1856 y 1956, de diversas provincias de Ecuador, particularmente de la región interandina, costa, noroccidente del país y la Amazonía hasta Cuyuja, y unas pocas de Europa. El Herbario Padre Luis Sodiro (QPLS) contiene alrededor de 13500 especímenes que representan aproximadamente 5500 especies de 254 familias botánicas." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/57a6e7b4-9fde-4efb-be3b-b800a6dd72f5> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-78.495555 -0.11146)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/57a6e7b4-9fde-4efb-be3b-b800a6dd72f5> <https://schema.org/logo> <https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019_BEAEP_Logo_fondo_transparente.png> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e2b05470-4927-4d35-8e6f-4b82fbd8e8e3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e2b05470-4927-4d35-8e6f-4b82fbd8e8e3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e2b05470-4927-4d35-8e6f-4b82fbd8e8e3> <https://schema.org/name> "Museo Náutico Farallón Dillon" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e2b05470-4927-4d35-8e6f-4b82fbd8e8e3> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "MNAFAD" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e2b05470-4927-4d35-8e6f-4b82fbd8e8e3> <https://schema.org/description> "The collection of specimens of the Farallon Dillon Nautical Museum is housed in the Chapel of the Virgen del Cisne de los Navegantes built by the owners of the Hostería Farallon Dillon in the 1990s. Here lie nautical items collected by Captain Alberto Dillon during his period as a merchant marine. The natural history pieces correspond to the bones of aquatic vertebrates, taxidermed sea turtles, coral and shells of different species. The collection has a use for environmental education since visits to museums by people from different parts of the world are important. The museum receives at least 10,000 visitors a year." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e2b05470-4927-4d35-8e6f-4b82fbd8e8e3> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-80.86374 -2.19852)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

