<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/705bdbcf-2f4c-47ca-983d-e7c704e16523> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/705bdbcf-2f4c-47ca-983d-e7c704e16523> <https://schema.org/name> "Historic England" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/705bdbcf-2f4c-47ca-983d-e7c704e16523> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "HE" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/705bdbcf-2f4c-47ca-983d-e7c704e16523> <https://schema.org/description> "Historic England is the public body that helps people care for, enjoy and celebrate England's spectacular historic environment. Our natural science collections originate in the work of the Ancient Monuments Laboratory, which was founded in 1931 and incorporated into the newly established Historic Buildings and Monuments Commission for England (HBMCE) in 1983. HBMCE was originally known as English Heritage, becoming Historic England in 2015, at which time the moniker ‘English Heritage’ moved to a newly formed charitable trust. The natural science collections are associated with Historic England’s Investigative Science Team, and many with Fort Cumberland Laboratories, Portsmouth, formally known as part of the Centre for Archaeology (1999-2005). They include comparative collections used for Environmental Archaeology (archaeobotany, archaeomalacology, geoarchaeology, human skeletal biology, palynology, zooarchaeology) Materials Science, Archaeological Conservation and Scientific Dating research. The collections primarily contain UK specimens, though a small number (" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/705bdbcf-2f4c-47ca-983d-e7c704e16523> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-1.032265 50.78884)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/76f330fc-6579-45d7-a21c-92603878a8bd> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/76f330fc-6579-45d7-a21c-92603878a8bd> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/76f330fc-6579-45d7-a21c-92603878a8bd> <https://schema.org/name> "Hastings Museum and Art Gallery" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/76f330fc-6579-45d7-a21c-92603878a8bd> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "HASMG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/76f330fc-6579-45d7-a21c-92603878a8bd> <https://schema.org/description> "The Hastings and St Leonards Museum Association founded Hastings Museum in 1890 and ownership was transferred to the County Borough of Hastings in 1905. Today, the museum is part of the Hastings Borough Council’s Regeneration and Culture Directorate and works within the context of a number of local strategies. The Association remain involved via the Museum Committee - an advisory Committee of the Cabinet of Hastings Borough Council that includes council members, one of whom is chair, and members of Hastings and St Leonards Museum Association. , , The museum’s collections include Fine Art, Ceramics, Local History, Archives, Natural History, Geology, Native American and World Collections. The collections continue to grow and currently numbers around 97,000 objects. Local history is displayed in three galleries: ‘Before Hastings’ tells the story of the area from prehistory to the Saxons, with ‘The Story of Hastings in 66 Objects’ and ‘Seaside’ galleries continuing the story from 1066 through to today. Natur.. The osteology collections are the most widespread geographically with specimens from Africa, Asia, Australasia, South America, Europe and Northern Polar regions., , The marine biology collection is mainly made up of material from around the UK (chiefly Hastings) but includes some foreign material from historical expeditions such as HMS Challenger (1872-1876). There are also a number of tropical shells from around the world., , The taxidermy collection is almost exclusively European and mostly from the south of the UK. , The fossil collection is thought to be exclusively UK material., The entomology collection is thought to be exclusively UK material." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/76f330fc-6579-45d7-a21c-92603878a8bd> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q15223260> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/76f330fc-6579-45d7-a21c-92603878a8bd> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(0.568568 50.85758)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

