<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2482559b-f103-4f35-8e75-e6119a4e2525> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2482559b-f103-4f35-8e75-e6119a4e2525> <https://schema.org/name> "Norwegian Institute for Nature Research" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2482559b-f103-4f35-8e75-e6119a4e2525> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NINA" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2482559b-f103-4f35-8e75-e6119a4e2525> <https://schema.org/description> "The Norwegian Institute for Nature Research (NINA) is Norway’s leading institution for applied ecological research, with broad-based expertise on the genetic, population, species, ecosystem and landscape level, in terrestrial, freshwater and coastal marine environments. The headquarters are located in Trondheim, with branches in Tromsø, Lillehammer, Bergen and Oslo in Norway. NINA’s scientists come from a wide range of disciplinary backgrounds that include biologists, geographers, geneticists, social scientists, sociologists and more. We have a broad-based expertise on the genetic, population, species, ecosystem and landscape level, in terrestrial, freshwater and coastal marine ecosystems." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2482559b-f103-4f35-8e75-e6119a4e2525> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11004196> .
