<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d0fd6677-a7ec-48a3-be9c-423ff40f2713> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d0fd6677-a7ec-48a3-be9c-423ff40f2713> <https://schema.org/name> "St. Thomas' College (Autonomous) Thrissur" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d0fd6677-a7ec-48a3-be9c-423ff40f2713> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "STC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d0fd6677-a7ec-48a3-be9c-423ff40f2713> <https://schema.org/description> "The St. Thomas College Herbarium was established after the starting of the Intermediate Course in 1952 and was formerly accredited with Index Herbariorum in January 2020. The herbarium housed more than 11,500 specimens from Kerala, and other South Indian states. including Algae, Fungi, Pteridophytes, Gymnosperms, and Plant Pathology. The web database includes a total of 1130 records representing 126 families consisting of Angiosperms (983 sheets), Algae (73), Pteridophytes (43), Fungi and Pathology (28), and Gymnosperms (3). The predominant plant families with maximum records represented are Gentianaceae (76 records), Asteraceae (53), Polygalaceae (41), Apocynaceae (39), Asclepiadaceae (38) and Meliaceae (38). Among these, more than 200 specimens were restored from their original damaged sheets by using handmade paper sheets. The restoration and digitization of the remaining sheets are under process. Western Ghats. Collections include Angiosperms, Algae, Pteridophytes, Fungi, Pathology and Gymnosperms" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d0fd6677-a7ec-48a3-be9c-423ff40f2713> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(76.219298 10.52897)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/1440952b-3c7a-497c-80e6-488a5b931f64> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/1440952b-3c7a-497c-80e6-488a5b931f64> <https://schema.org/name> "Qarshi Botanical Garden, Qarshi Industries Pvt. Ltd, Pakistan" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/1440952b-3c7a-497c-80e6-488a5b931f64> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "QARSHI" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/1440952b-3c7a-497c-80e6-488a5b931f64> <https://schema.org/description> "Qarshi Botanical Garden (QBG) was established in 2000 with the aim to conserve and propagate commercially important indigenous plant species.Qarshi Botanical Garden (QBG) is situated on the premises of Qarshi Industries (Pvt.) Ltd, which is located in the industrial Estate, Hattar, District Haripur, KPK, Pakistan on Taxila - Haripur Road. The Herb Garden is spread over 4 acres.The Herb Garden is tastefully designed and laid out for planting of medicinal plant species. It contains more than 276 medicinal plant species of 52 families and 212 genera. There are 56 exotic species including herbs, shrubs & trees. Some of important exotic species are Ginkgo biloba, Hibiscus sabdariffa, Stevia rebaudiana, Zephranthes grandiflora, Aloe barbadensis, Helianthus tuberosus, Nepeta cataria, Mentha spicata, Cymbopogon citratus, Plantago lanceolata, Plumbago zeylanica, Rosmarinus officinalis, Hyssopus officinalis, Melissa officinalis,Cynara scolymus, Ribis grossularis etc. Rests of species are indigenous and collected from different ecological zones of Pakistan especially mountainous regions including Northern Areas and Azad Jammu and Kashmir. The important indigenous species planted include Lawsonia alba, Achillia millifolium, Ajuga reptans, Althea rosea, Berberis lycium, Cichorium intybus, Fragaria vesca, Podophyllum emodi, Cymbopogon citratus, Tanacetum parthenium, Mirabilis jalapa, Crategus fragrans, Calotropus procera, Glycchariza glabra, Ocimum bacilicum, Rosa damascene, Mentha arvensis, Curcuma longa, Sida cordifolia, Plantago major, Viola odorata. Some threatened species like Taxus wallichiana, Withania somnifera, Valariana jatamansi, Juniperus excelsa, Murraya koenigii, Berginia cilliata have also been planted in the Herb Garden for conservation & propagation. There are 16 species of Cactus and 7 species of Juniper.Each plant species planted in the Herb Garden is labeled in standard format which provides all relevant information about plant species such as Urdu name, Botanical name, English name, Family, Part used, Medicinal use / Action etc." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/1440952b-3c7a-497c-80e6-488a5b931f64> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(72.861396 33.897465)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

