<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d347ee2a-e411-4c9a-9f15-bbaf037057bc> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d347ee2a-e411-4c9a-9f15-bbaf037057bc> <https://schema.org/name> "Institute of Ecology and Biological Resources" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d347ee2a-e411-4c9a-9f15-bbaf037057bc> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "IEBR" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/d347ee2a-e411-4c9a-9f15-bbaf037057bc> <https://schema.org/description> "Innstitute of Ecology and Biological Resources is a leading institution in Vietnam in Life Science. It contains one of the largest collections of animals and plants collected from the country and surroundings." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5a4d350-04e5-43fe-90ca-53cd7780234a> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5a4d350-04e5-43fe-90ca-53cd7780234a> <https://schema.org/name> "Taxon Expeditions" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5a4d350-04e5-43fe-90ca-53cd7780234a> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "TXEX" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a5a4d350-04e5-43fe-90ca-53cd7780234a> <https://schema.org/description> "Taxon Expeditions B.V. organises field courses and expeditions for non-biologists, with the specific aim to involve citizen scientists in biodiscovery. Course participants discover, curate, examine, and publish new species, particularly invertebrate animals. Taxonomic papers resulting from this work are published within the Taxon Expeditions collection of the Biodiversity Data Journal. When permitted by the authorities of the field sites, samples are stored in the Taxon Expeditions collection, based in Leiden, the Netherlands. The Taxon Expeditions collection is also listed in The Insect and Spider Collections of the World." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af0a9c21-80ab-48bd-a266-9ffc60dc3353> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af0a9c21-80ab-48bd-a266-9ffc60dc3353> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af0a9c21-80ab-48bd-a266-9ffc60dc3353> <https://schema.org/name> "The Bacteriophage Bank of Korea" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af0a9c21-80ab-48bd-a266-9ffc60dc3353> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "HUFS" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af0a9c21-80ab-48bd-a266-9ffc60dc3353> <https://schema.org/description> "We collect, stock, and distribute bacteriophages. Currently in 2017, we have more than 2,000 phages infecting 30+ different host bacteria in stock." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/8e22911b-9d12-4730-81b0-ba170cafd9d8> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/8e22911b-9d12-4730-81b0-ba170cafd9d8> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/8e22911b-9d12-4730-81b0-ba170cafd9d8> <https://schema.org/name> "Broward College" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/8e22911b-9d12-4730-81b0-ba170cafd9d8> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "BROW" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/8e22911b-9d12-4730-81b0-ba170cafd9d8> <https://schema.org/description> "The focus of the Broward College Insect Collection is to house and make available arthropod collections focused on South Flrodia and the greatere Everglades ecosystems. Esthablished in 2017, it began with about 30,000 specimens from the faculty&#39;s personal/research collections and historic student/teaching collections of the college. Donations and student survey projects currently feed the collection. For further information and to arrange a visit or loan, please contact collection director Dr. David Serrano (dserrano@broward.edu)." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/8e22911b-9d12-4730-81b0-ba170cafd9d8> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/02jj1w915> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/8e22911b-9d12-4730-81b0-ba170cafd9d8> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-80.23441 26.08031)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/71fabe61-7a7d-42a2-a162-d22784b080cd> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/71fabe61-7a7d-42a2-a162-d22784b080cd> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/71fabe61-7a7d-42a2-a162-d22784b080cd> <https://schema.org/name> "Mie Prefectural Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/71fabe61-7a7d-42a2-a162-d22784b080cd> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "MIE" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/71fabe61-7a7d-42a2-a162-d22784b080cd> <https://schema.org/description> "Mie Prefectural Museum is home to more than 500,000 documents and artifacts related to Mie&rsquo;s natural landscape, history, and culture. With an interactive area, an exhibit area that features Mie&rsquo;s distinct characteristics and the &ldquo;Museum Field&rdquo;, where visitors can observe Mie&rsquo;s seasonal changes, we are sure to stimulate the mind with each visit. We hope that by discovering more about Mie, visitors come to realize the strength of Mie&rsquo;s diversity." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/71fabe61-7a7d-42a2-a162-d22784b080cd> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/03pwtnm85> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/71fabe61-7a7d-42a2-a162-d22784b080cd> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(136.501667 34.742222)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/262c6b69-0d4b-4963-aa55-3c6f93101816> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/262c6b69-0d4b-4963-aa55-3c6f93101816> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/262c6b69-0d4b-4963-aa55-3c6f93101816> <https://schema.org/name> "Bailey-Matthews National Shell Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/262c6b69-0d4b-4963-aa55-3c6f93101816> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "BMNSM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/262c6b69-0d4b-4963-aa55-3c6f93101816> <https://schema.org/description> "The Bailey-Matthews National Shell Museum is a museum devoted to every aspect of seashells, conchology, and malacology, including the paleontological and archeological/anthropological aspects of the study of shells. The museum is located in the city of Sanibel on Sanibel Island on the Gulf of Mexico coast of Southwest Florida.[1] The Bailey-Matthews National Shell Museum opened to the public in 1995, and operates as an information and reference center for national and international scientists, students, and shell enthusiasts, particularly those who are interested in the marine, terrestrial, and freshwater mollusks of the Gulf of Mexico and Florida. The Museum, which is a facility of the Shell Museum and Educational Foundation, Inc., received its first accreditation from the American Alliance of Museums in March 2010. In addition to its 40+ exhibits, public programs, and in-house live-mollusk demonstrations, the museum has embarked on many collaborations with national and international educational and research institutions, and offers facilities in its collection and research area for visiting researchers, interns, and students. Resources are used by national and international professionals in the fields of environmental and marine sciences, biology, and ecology. The Museum also offers a formal field trip program for Lee County public school 4th-graders on a cost-sharing basis. The Museum has established strong ties with many public and private sector organizations, including the United Nations&rsquo; Food and Agricultural Organization, Smithsonian Institution, American Malacological Society, Conchologists of America, Museu de Zoologia and Universidade de S&atilde;o Paulo in Brazil, Florida Museum of Natural History, Southwest Florida Library Network, Sanibel Public Library, The Sanibel School, Sanibel-Captiva Chamber of Commerce, Lee County&rsquo;s Visitor and Convention Bureau and the Tourism and Development Council, and shell clubs throughout Florida. The museum&#39;s website includes database searches for both the museum and southwestern Florida. The museum runs programs to educate the public, such as guided beach walks with museum naturalists who identify shells and teach mollusk biology.[4] History[edit]" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/262c6b69-0d4b-4963-aa55-3c6f93101816> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q4848325> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/6b09e583-5c1d-4463-855c-40de722ad84e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/6b09e583-5c1d-4463-855c-40de722ad84e> <https://schema.org/name> "University of New Mexico Earth Science Collections" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/6b09e583-5c1d-4463-855c-40de722ad84e> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "UNMES" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/6b09e583-5c1d-4463-855c-40de722ad84e> <https://schema.org/description> "The University of New Mexico Earth Science Collections includes a variety of paleobiological, rock, and mineral specimens. The paleobiology collection (UNMP) includes over 25,000 specimens, primarily of marine invertebrate and terrestrial plant fossils. The collection spans the entire Phanerozoic Eon and the geographic scope is global, with an emphasis on specimens from New Mexico and the surrounding region. Many of these regional specimens are not well represented in other academic or national museum collections. Flora and fauna from the Carboniferous, Permian, and Cretaceous Periods are particularly well represented. The collection also includes more than 50 type specimens of Pennsylvanian and Early Cretaceous mollusks, and Pennsylvanian insects. Preservation of specimens is primarily as body or trace fossils. The petrologic and mineral collections consist of over 20,000 specimens spanning from the Proterozoic to Modern and containing notable specimens from the Southwest region and globally. These collections include unique petrologic collections from the Harding Pegmatite Mine, NM and Chuar Group from the Grand Canyon region. Preservation of specimens is primarily as hand samples, thin sections, and rock/mineral powders." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/6b09e583-5c1d-4463-855c-40de722ad84e> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/05fs6jp91> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c034ea21-2f89-4c53-acf2-40ba2eec123e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c034ea21-2f89-4c53-acf2-40ba2eec123e> <https://schema.org/name> "Instituto de Biología Subtropical" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c034ea21-2f89-4c53-acf2-40ba2eec123e> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "IBSI" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c034ea21-2f89-4c53-acf2-40ba2eec123e> <https://schema.org/description> "Instituto de Biolog&iacute;a Subtropical - Iguaz&uacute; El Instituto de Biolog&iacute;a Subtropical (IBS) es una unidad ejecutora de investigaciones cient&iacute;ficas y tecnol&oacute;gicas de doble dependencia y est&aacute; situado en la Provincia de Misiones, Argentina. Depende de la Universidad Nacional de Misiones (UNaM) y del Consejo Nacional de Investigaciones Cient&iacute;ficas y T&eacute;cnicas (CONICET). Actualmente cuenta con dos sedes, una en Posadas, que funciona en dependencias de la Facultad de Ciencias Exactas, Qu&iacute;micas y Naturales, y la otra en Puerto Iguaz&uacute;, que est&aacute; relacionada acad&eacute;micamente a la Facultad de Ciencias Forestales de la UNaM. El IBS es un instituto destinado a generar y transferir conocimiento cient&iacute;fico en biolog&iacute;a b&aacute;sica y aplicada, con especial &eacute;nfasis en estudios sobre gen&eacute;tica, biodiversidad, biolog&iacute;a de la conservaci&oacute;n y sobre el funcionamiento y el manejo de los ecosistemas subtropicales de Argentina. Las actividades de investigaci&oacute;n del IBS-Iguaz&uacute; se desarrollan en el marco de estudios integrales y multidisciplinarios, incluyendo aspectos sociales y econ&oacute;micos. La finalidad del IBS Iguaz&uacute; es generar conocimiento cient&iacute;fico b&aacute;sico y dar respuestas adecuadas a las demandas de los distintos sectores sociales vinculados con el uso y el manejo de los recursos naturales y culturales, para contribuir y promover al desarrollo sustentable de la regi&oacute;n. En el IBS Iguaz&uacute; hay seis grupos de investigaci&oacute;n, los cuales est&aacute;n integrados por investigadores, becarios, tesistas, t&eacute;cnicos, pasantes y voluntarios que desarrollan uno o m&aacute;s proyectos de investigaci&oacute;n dentro de una tem&aacute;tica general. Los principales grupos de investigaci&oacute;n que desarrollan sus actividades en el instituto son: 1- Grupo de Etnobiolog&iacute;a (Etno IBS); 2- Grupo de Ecolog&iacute;a y Funcionamiento de Comunidades y Ecosistemas (GEFuCE); 3- Grupo de Ecolog&iacute;a y Conservaci&oacute;n de Mam&iacute;feros; 4- Grupo de Ecolog&iacute;a Forestal y Ecofisiolog&iacute;a; 5- Grupo de Ictiolog&iacute;a y Ecolog&iacute;a Acu&aacute;tica; y, 6- Grupo de Gen&eacute;tica de la Conservaci&oacute;n. Actualmente el instituto cuenta con diez colecciones biol&oacute;gicas, son &eacute;stas: 1- Colecci&oacute;n fotogr&aacute;fica (IBSI Fot); 2- Colecci&oacute;n de Plantas de Importancia Cultural (IBSI Herb); 3- Palinoteca de referencia de Bosques Subtropicales (IBSI Pal); 4- Colecci&oacute;n de semillas (IBSI Ger); 5- Colecci&oacute;n de Micro-Artr&oacute;podos (IBSI MA); 6- Colecci&oacute;n Araneol&oacute;gica (IBSI Ara); 7- Colecci&oacute;n de Apiformes (IBSI Api); 8- Colecci&oacute;n de escarabeidos (IBSI Sca); 9- Colecci&oacute;n Ictiol&oacute;gica (IBSI Ict); 10- Colecci&oacute;n de Mam&iacute;feros (IBSI Mam)." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c034ea21-2f89-4c53-acf2-40ba2eec123e> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/04jdqdr13> .

