<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a1bec049-87d1-40ee-bc5f-22d2c511cf4b> <https://schema.org/name> "Sugadaira Research Station, Mountain Science Center, University of Tsukuba" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a1bec049-87d1-40ee-bc5f-22d2c511cf4b> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "SUG" .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/27bcef1d-2467-41c5-8cb6-8acd1fcc513e> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/27bcef1d-2467-41c5-8cb6-8acd1fcc513e> <https://schema.org/name> "Istituto di Scienze Marine di Venezia" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/27bcef1d-2467-41c5-8cb6-8acd1fcc513e> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "CNR-ISMAR-VE" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/27bcef1d-2467-41c5-8cb6-8acd1fcc513e> <https://schema.org/description> "The Institute of Marine Sciences, National Research Council of Italy (CNR-ISMAR) conducts basic and applied research in biological, physical and chemical oceanography and marine geology with the aim of contributing both to the study of ocean processes and climate variability and to the development of systems/services for the observation, protection and sustainable management of the marine and coastal environment. CNR-ISMAR host the Herbarium ISMAR and collections of marine invertebrates from different Mediterranean and non-Mediterranean geographical areas. CNR-ISMAR join to the European Research Infrastructure \"DiSSCo\"." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/27bcef1d-2467-41c5-8cb6-8acd1fcc513e> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/02hdf6119> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/27bcef1d-2467-41c5-8cb6-8acd1fcc513e> <https://schema.org/logo> <https://www.cnr.it/it/istituto/080/logo> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3c56fe8d-99f6-4638-bb20-73a2e8f799c5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3c56fe8d-99f6-4638-bb20-73a2e8f799c5> <https://schema.org/name> "NSW Plant Pathology & Mycology Culture Collection" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3c56fe8d-99f6-4638-bb20-73a2e8f799c5> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "DARCC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3c56fe8d-99f6-4638-bb20-73a2e8f799c5> <https://schema.org/description> "The NSW Plant Pathology &amp; Mycology Herbarium, of the Biosecurity Collections, holds diseases of plants from Australia and overseas including fungi, bacteria, viruses and nematodes. The collection houses many important specimens to both Australian agriculture and biosecurity and to scientific research. It is the largest of its kind in Australia, incredibly important to Australian industry, and the environment, and is irreplaceable. The collection primarily holds plant diseases i.e. a pathogen that is injurious to plant health, plant products or plant beneficial organisms (such as diseases of plant beneficial insects or fungi). We also have non-pathogenic fungi (like mushrooms and lichen) and we are in fact the only recognised mycology (fungi) collection in NSW. In addition to the preserved dried specimen we also maintain living cultures of plant pathogens and other fungi and bacteria, such as beneficial or commercially important species and strains. We have over 6,000 cultures, some dating as far back as the 1970s." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b407b9c3-b644-47e5-8888-b75bf4384e8b> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b407b9c3-b644-47e5-8888-b75bf4384e8b> <https://schema.org/name> "HHS/NIH, National Institutes of Health" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b407b9c3-b644-47e5-8888-b75bf4384e8b> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NIH" .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0f2d64f3-b817-46c0-a0f3-38e30847ebfe> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0f2d64f3-b817-46c0-a0f3-38e30847ebfe> <https://schema.org/name> "Smithsonian Tropical Research Institute" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0f2d64f3-b817-46c0-a0f3-38e30847ebfe> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "STRI" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0f2d64f3-b817-46c0-a0f3-38e30847ebfe> <https://schema.org/description> "The Smithsonian Tropical Research Institution (STRI) in Panama, is a bureau of the Smithsonian Institution based outside of the United States, is dedicated to understanding biological diversity. STRI aims to offer research facilities that allow staff scientists, fellows, and visiting scientists to achieve their research objectives. The 38 staff scientists reside in the tropics and are encouraged to pursue their own research priorities without geographic limitations. The continuity of their long-term programs enables in-depth investigations that attract an elite group of fellows and visitors. Active support for fellows and visitors leverages resources further and attracts more than 900 scientists to STRI each year. Although STRI is based in Panama, research is conducted throughout the tropics. STRI&#39;s Center for Tropical Forest Science uses large, fully enumerated forest plots to monitor tree demography in 14 countries located in Africa, Asia and the Americas. More than 3,000,000 individual trees representing 6,000 species are being studied. STRI&#39;s Biological Diversity of Forest Fragments project created experimental forest fragments of 0.01, 0.1, and 1.0 km 2 to study the consequences of landscape transformation on forest integrity in the central Amazon region. STRI marine scientists are conducting a global survey of levels of genetic isolation in coral reef organisms. Barro Colorado Island." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0f2d64f3-b817-46c0-a0f3-38e30847ebfe> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/035jbxr46> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0f2d64f3-b817-46c0-a0f3-38e30847ebfe> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1673980> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0f2d64f3-b817-46c0-a0f3-38e30847ebfe> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-79.543706 8.962998)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f29891c5-e66d-47f2-ad42-78c5ed688264> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f29891c5-e66d-47f2-ad42-78c5ed688264> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f29891c5-e66d-47f2-ad42-78c5ed688264> <https://schema.org/name> "Gunma Insect World" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f29891c5-e66d-47f2-ad42-78c5ed688264> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "GIW" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f29891c5-e66d-47f2-ad42-78c5ed688264> <https://schema.org/description> "Our Institution is Insectarium with Entomological park in the 45 hectare site, including groves of trees, rice and cultivated fieds, etc." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/273fc670-1eec-4dfe-87f3-ff31ad0e2685> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/273fc670-1eec-4dfe-87f3-ff31ad0e2685> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/273fc670-1eec-4dfe-87f3-ff31ad0e2685> <https://schema.org/name> "Johnston State College" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/273fc670-1eec-4dfe-87f3-ff31ad0e2685> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NVUJ" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/273fc670-1eec-4dfe-87f3-ff31ad0e2685> <https://schema.org/description> "Johnson State College* believes in the power of a liberal arts education to transform lives. We express this belief by providing high-impact, interdisciplinary learning experiences that cross academic and experiential boundaries; by creating opportunities for students to extend their classroom learning to the field, the laboratory, the studio, the community and the local and wider world; by recognizing and supporting the diverse starting points, backgrounds and goals of students; and by sustaining high standards, active participation, vigorous debate and mutual respect. The Johnson State College Collection represents decades of work by faculty and students in sampling the biodiversity of the region. While the collection includes a range of specimens including mammal skulls and bird skins, the bulk is made up of invertabrates. *Johnston State College will become Northern Vermont University - Johnson effective July 1, 2018." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/320dd2df-9fa8-4357-874d-314ab7449892> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/320dd2df-9fa8-4357-874d-314ab7449892> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/320dd2df-9fa8-4357-874d-314ab7449892> <https://schema.org/name> "San Diego State University" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/320dd2df-9fa8-4357-874d-314ab7449892> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "SDSU-MoB" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/320dd2df-9fa8-4357-874d-314ab7449892> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q913861> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/7fa92a67-a8c4-4f7d-a27f-ba24b1b2b698> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/7fa92a67-a8c4-4f7d-a27f-ba24b1b2b698> <https://schema.org/name> "DOI/USGS, Pacific Region Menlo Park Sample Repository" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/7fa92a67-a8c4-4f7d-a27f-ba24b1b2b698> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "USGSMP" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/7fa92a67-a8c4-4f7d-a27f-ba24b1b2b698> <https://schema.org/description> "The US Geological Survey (USGS), Pacific Region, Menlo Park Sample Repository is located in 4 warehouses located at 1020, 1030, 1040, and 1050 O&#39;Brien Drive, Menlo Park Ca, 94025. The warehouses (https://walrus.wr.usgs.gov/infobank/programs/html/facilities/ob/ob.campus.html) house an array of geologic samples, and marine analog and digital data associated with the marine portions of the collection. The collection is composed samples (terresrtial and marine) collected in the over 60+ year existence of the USGS in Menlo Park. Although the database is out of date, we have the samples well packed, safely stored, and easily accesible to all the research commounity. Another link to the collection that may be of use is: https://walrus.wr.usgs.gov/infobank/programs/html/main/activities.html Contacts include: Michael Torresan (mtorresan@usgs.gov) Carol Reiss (creiss@usgs.gov) Emeritus Jon Childs (jchilds@usgs.gov)" .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/445d9d62-2df9-48cd-b7dc-edbaa1af1ad9> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/445d9d62-2df9-48cd-b7dc-edbaa1af1ad9> <https://schema.org/name> "National Institute of Child Health and Human Development" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/445d9d62-2df9-48cd-b7dc-edbaa1af1ad9> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NICHD" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/445d9d62-2df9-48cd-b7dc-edbaa1af1ad9> <https://schema.org/description> "NICHD was established in 1962 by President John F. Kennedy, with the support of Congress, to study the &quot;complex process of human development from conception to old age. NICHD is one of 27 institutes and centers that comprise the National Institutes of Health, the largest biomedical research agency in the world, part of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. In pursuit of its mission, NICHD conducts and supports laboratory research, clinical trials, and epidemiological studies that explore health processes; examines the impact of disability and disease; and sponsors training programs for scientists, health care providers, and researchers​." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af47e5c1-6492-4dd1-bbbd-23132e849144> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af47e5c1-6492-4dd1-bbbd-23132e849144> <https://schema.org/name> "DOI/USGS, Pacific Coastal and Marine Science Center Samples Repository" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af47e5c1-6492-4dd1-bbbd-23132e849144> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "PCMSC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/af47e5c1-6492-4dd1-bbbd-23132e849144> <https://schema.org/description> "We are the US Geological Survey (USGS), Pacific Coastal and Marine Science Center (PCMSC). We conduct multidisciplinary scientific research in the coastal and offshore areas of California, Oregon, Washington, Alaska, Hawaii, and other US Pacific Islands; and in other waterways of the United States.(https://walrus.wr.usgs.gov). Much orf our work focusses on marine and coastal geology and oceanography, but we partner with researchers and collabroators in other fields. PCMSC is part of the USGS Coastal and Marine Geology Program (CMGP - https://marine.usgs.gov/about.html). As a program CMGP works with many and varied partners to ensure that our Nation has the information it needs to understand, restore and protect healthy coastal and ocean resources and the communities who depend on them. More than half of our Nation&#39;s population lives within 50 miles of the coast, along productive estuaries and extensive coastlines of the Gulf of Mexico, the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans, and the Caribbean Sea. Healthy coastal and offshore resources are vital to our nation&#39;s economy through energy, commerce and shipping, fisheries, tourism and recreation. We rely on new technologies, scientific data and analysis to support the wise use and protection of these resources and also inform policies and decisions that prepare us for extreme events, natural hazards, and climate change. Coastal and marine geology expertise contributes to the greater USGS mission of providing impartial information on the health of our ecosystems and environment, the natural hazards that threaten us, the natural resources we rely on, the impacts of climate and land-use change, and the core science systems that help us provide timely, relevant, and usable information. The major CMGP Science Centers are located in Woods Hole, Massachusetts; Santa Cruz and Menlo Park, California; and St. Petersburg, Florida. Administrative offices are in Reston, Virginia. Part of our research results in the collection and subsequent preservation of analog and digital data records associated with our field work and the collection and preservation of samples that include sediment and drill cores, grabs, rocks. NOTE: If you are internal to usgs you can link to our sample data base by going to: http://compass.er.usgs.gov/sc/index.php. NOTE : If you are outside the USGS then you can go to: gov.noaa.ngdc.mgg.geology:archived-data_USGS-PCMSC-sample-repository" .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dd87411c-3c31-4014-91f0-d95825a5c37f> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dd87411c-3c31-4014-91f0-d95825a5c37f> <https://schema.org/name> "DOI/USGS, Saint Petersburg Coastal and Marine Science Center" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dd87411c-3c31-4014-91f0-d95825a5c37f> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "SPCMSC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dd87411c-3c31-4014-91f0-d95825a5c37f> <https://schema.org/description> "The Saint Petersburg Coastal and Marine Science Center (SPCMSC) is one of three USGS coastal and marine science centers in the Untied States, the other two being the Pacific Coastal and Marine Science Center (PCMSC) and the Woods Hole Coastal and Marine Science Center (WHCMSC). The SPCMSC holds geological samples from all over the United States, with the bulk of the samples residing from the Gulf of Mexico, and the lakes, rivers and coastal areas of Florida. The center is located in downtown Saint Petersburg, FL, on the University of South Florida, St. Petersburg Campus." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/41f92c09-fe46-44a5-9b1a-920fe2cc83eb> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/41f92c09-fe46-44a5-9b1a-920fe2cc83eb> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/41f92c09-fe46-44a5-9b1a-920fe2cc83eb> <https://schema.org/name> "University of Rochester" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/41f92c09-fe46-44a5-9b1a-920fe2cc83eb> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "UR" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/41f92c09-fe46-44a5-9b1a-920fe2cc83eb> <https://schema.org/description> "The University of Rochester has biological, mineralogical, and fossil material that had been part of the University of Rochester Natural Science Museum, a large museum active from 1860 until the late 1960&#39;s. Biological specimens and some fossils are housed in the Department of Biology, and minerals and many fossils are maintained by the Department of Earth and Environmental Sciences." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/41f92c09-fe46-44a5-9b1a-920fe2cc83eb> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q149990> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e195543b-f365-4a9e-ae47-c1bd5cc1f443> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e195543b-f365-4a9e-ae47-c1bd5cc1f443> <https://schema.org/name> "Senckenberg Institute for Plant Form and Function at Friedrich Schiller University Jena" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e195543b-f365-4a9e-ae47-c1bd5cc1f443> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "JE" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e195543b-f365-4a9e-ae47-c1bd5cc1f443> <https://schema.org/description> "Founded by Carl Haussknecht (1838–1903) in Weimar in 1896, the Herbarium Haussknecht was initially a private institution comprising the vast collections brought together by its founder in a separate building. After Haussknecht’s death in 1903 and execution of a foundation from his estate, the collections were maintained and increased by Joseph Bornmüller (1862–1948). After World War II, the collections were moved to Jena and were affiliated to the Institute of Systematic Botany of the Friedrich Schiller University, to which they officially belonged since the 1920s. Actually, the Herbarium Haussknecht houses about 3.5 Million herbarium specimens, being one of the major European herbaria. Carl Haussknecht started plant collecting not earlier than in the mid-1850s. Compared with the size of the entire collection, there is a rather low number of older material at JE. Such historical collections include for example the herbaria of Christian Ehrenfried Weigel (1748–1831) and Johann Friedrich Glaser (1707–1789), which are kept separately. Other collections containing large amounts of old material are the herbaria of Charles Gaillardot (1814–1883) and Wilhelm Gerhard (1780–1858), both acquired by Haussknecht, and those of Alwin Georges (1823–1893) and Thilo Irmisch (1816–1879). Besides the widely distributed exsiccata, like those of Friedrich Metz (1819–1885) and Franz Sieber (1789–1844), these collections include even rarer material, for example specimens collected by Friedrich August Marschall von Bieberstein (1768–1826), Anton Rochel (1770–1847), and Johan Peter Rottler (1749–1836)." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e195543b-f365-4a9e-ae47-c1bd5cc1f443> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q154561> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e195543b-f365-4a9e-ae47-c1bd5cc1f443> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/05qpz1x62> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/e195543b-f365-4a9e-ae47-c1bd5cc1f443> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(11.5902 50.92966)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c5267ab2-d499-4eab-98c5-6b6b2764d17d> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c5267ab2-d499-4eab-98c5-6b6b2764d17d> <https://schema.org/name> "University of California-Berkeley, Sagehen Creek Field Station" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c5267ab2-d499-4eab-98c5-6b6b2764d17d> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "SCFS" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c5267ab2-d499-4eab-98c5-6b6b2764d17d> <https://schema.org/description> "Sagehen Creek Field Station is a research and teaching facility of the University of California at Berkeley. Established in 1951, the field station sits within the 9,000-acre Sagehen Experimental Forest. Sagehen maintains teaching collections of plants, mammals, birds and insects. These collections began in the 1950&#39;s. While the animals are inactive, the herbarium is actively being expanded with additions from the Sagehen basin and the properties of the other Central Sierra Field Research Stations in the North Fork of the American River. Sagehen also engages volunteers to collect geo-referenced observations through iNaturalist to complement the physical collections." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c5267ab2-d499-4eab-98c5-6b6b2764d17d> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/01an7q238> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/c5267ab2-d499-4eab-98c5-6b6b2764d17d> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(-120.2411 39.432)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/45e9fad9-c756-4d76-84b0-480faa9fd523> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/45e9fad9-c756-4d76-84b0-480faa9fd523> <https://schema.org/name> "Omachi Alpine Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/45e9fad9-c756-4d76-84b0-480faa9fd523> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "OAM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/45e9fad9-c756-4d76-84b0-480faa9fd523> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11437989> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/89e05d54-df78-473e-bfef-09fd5b93188a> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/89e05d54-df78-473e-bfef-09fd5b93188a> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/89e05d54-df78-473e-bfef-09fd5b93188a> <https://schema.org/name> "Kushiro City Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/89e05d54-df78-473e-bfef-09fd5b93188a> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "KCMH" .
