<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3249add6-3120-42c4-bbd8-1c7d76f02a38> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3249add6-3120-42c4-bbd8-1c7d76f02a38> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3249add6-3120-42c4-bbd8-1c7d76f02a38> <https://schema.org/name> "Tallahassee Museum of History & Natural Science" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3249add6-3120-42c4-bbd8-1c7d76f02a38> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "TMHNS" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3249add6-3120-42c4-bbd8-1c7d76f02a38> <https://schema.org/description> "Our mission is to promote knowledge and understanding of our region&#39;s cultural history and natural environment. The collections provide specimens and artifacts as resources for exhibit development and educational programming. Most of the natural history items are currently, or were historically, native to the region. Specimens from other regions are used to contrast and compare." .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/00e8f85c-106e-4ffe-9b3a-d6f9e647094c> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/00e8f85c-106e-4ffe-9b3a-d6f9e647094c> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/00e8f85c-106e-4ffe-9b3a-d6f9e647094c> <https://schema.org/name> "Southwestern Adventist University" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/00e8f85c-106e-4ffe-9b3a-d6f9e647094c> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "HRS" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/00e8f85c-106e-4ffe-9b3a-d6f9e647094c> <https://schema.org/description> "Present contents include more than 20,000 specimens, largely dinosaur bones, and associated metadata, photographs, site maps and 3DVR images of the bones. These are largely derived from the Upper Cretaceous Lance Formation in eastern Wyoming, where active excavation is ongoing." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/00e8f85c-106e-4ffe-9b3a-d6f9e647094c> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1056856> .
