<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b4e1522d-9a22-427f-afa1-b42b489117f5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b4e1522d-9a22-427f-afa1-b42b489117f5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/CollegeOrUniversity> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b4e1522d-9a22-427f-afa1-b42b489117f5> <https://schema.org/name> "Texas A&M University Insect Collection" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b4e1522d-9a22-427f-afa1-b42b489117f5> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "TAMUIC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/b4e1522d-9a22-427f-afa1-b42b489117f5> <https://schema.org/description> "Arthropod research collection; primarily terrestrial and aquatic insects; smaller numbers of terrestrial and aquatic non-insect arthropods; geographic strengths: southern (particularly south-central) United States and Mexico; size: ca. 2.8 million curated specimens (2016)" .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <https://schema.org/name> "Ditsong National Museum of Natural History" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "DNMNH" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <https://schema.org/description> "The Ditsong National Museum of Natural History, formerly the Transvaal Museum, was founded in 1892 and is situated in Pretoria, South Africa. It forms part of DITSONG: Museums of South Africa. The zoological collections contain insects and other invertebrates, reptiles, amphibians, birds and mammals, primarily from southern Africa. The museum houses the largest Coleoptera and herpetology collections in Africa. Its zoological type collections are among the largest and most diverse in South Africa, with an estimated 19,351 specimens representing 13,314 species. The museum also holds an archaeozoological comparative collection and Karoo and Plio-Pleistocene fossil collections. The Karoo collection includes late Permian therapsids. The Plio-Pleistocene collection contains hominins and associated fauna from Sterkfontein, Swartkrans, Kromdraai and other sites in the Cradle of Humankind World Heritage Site, including the Australopithecus africanus skull known as \"Mrs Ples\"." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2583379> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(28.1893 -25.7531)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/11983b3c-cb83-40d5-95d9-17fd17503ab2> <https://schema.org/logo> <https://ditsong.org.za/en/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/logo2.png> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a47046da-867a-4a59-a0c2-5747223ffdc8> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a47046da-867a-4a59-a0c2-5747223ffdc8> <https://schema.org/name> "Thailand Bioresource Research Center" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a47046da-867a-4a59-a0c2-5747223ffdc8> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "TBRC" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/a47046da-867a-4a59-a0c2-5747223ffdc8> <https://schema.org/description> "The TBRC Network facilitates the coordination of exchange of biological information and resources and develops mechanisms enabled by information technologies to broaden access to biomaterials to the public and scientific community." .
