<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3ed3bab0-81a5-49bc-bd4e-9daeb556f33c> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3ed3bab0-81a5-49bc-bd4e-9daeb556f33c> <https://schema.org/name> "Teylers Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3ed3bab0-81a5-49bc-bd4e-9daeb556f33c> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "TM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3ed3bab0-81a5-49bc-bd4e-9daeb556f33c> <https://schema.org/description> "Teylers Museum opened its doors in 1784 and is now known as the oldest museum in the Netherlands. Ancient fossils, magnificent stones, ingenious devices, precious books, fascinating coins and medals, fabulous drawings, and romantic paintings: the collection is unique in the world, combining art and science. Teylers Museum's collection, comprising drawings and prints, paintings, numismatics, fossils, minerals, instruments, and a natural science library, is of international significance from both a content and historical perspective. The paleontological collection of Teylers Museum includes over 22,000 specimens of all groups of animals, plants and microfossils. It is a historical collection containing many type specimens: it was largely collected between 1780 and 1950. It also includes material from sites that have long been closed or are have nowadays very limited access, such as the Dutch St. Pietersberg near Maastricht and limestone quarries near the Bavarian Solnhofen. In 1855, a specimen of a primitive bird (Ostromia crassipes) was found in this Solnhofen limestone formation. It is the oldest bird-like dinosaur outside Asia and the only known specimen to date, and is known as \"the Haarlem specimen”." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3ed3bab0-81a5-49bc-bd4e-9daeb556f33c> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q474563> .
