<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2203990f-7e07-4d23-aaa2-7acf1c39e348> <https://schema.org/name> "Zimbabwe Museum of Human Sciences" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2203990f-7e07-4d23-aaa2-7acf1c39e348> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "QVG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/2203990f-7e07-4d23-aaa2-7acf1c39e348> <https://schema.org/description> "The Zimbabwe Museum of Human Sciences, once known as the Queen Victoria Museum, is a museum located in Harare, Zimbabwe. This museum is particularly notable for housing the seven-hundred-year-old Lemba artifact, ngoma lungundu, believed by some to be a replica of the biblical Ark of the Covenant. This artifact is the oldest wooden object ever discovered in sub-Saharan Africa. The ngoma lungundu holds significant cultural importance for the Lemba people—black Africans who claim Jewish ancestry. The Lemba people suggest that this vessel was constructed nearly seven centuries ago, using remnants of the original Ark, which, according to biblical accounts, was used to store Moses' 10 Commandments. For many years, this ancient artifact was thought to be lost until its rediscovery in a Harare storeroom." .

