<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/571a17f5-b06d-4fc8-8c74-a18ff480b304> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/571a17f5-b06d-4fc8-8c74-a18ff480b304> <https://schema.org/name> "Museu de Zoologia da Universidade de Sao Paulo" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/571a17f5-b06d-4fc8-8c74-a18ff480b304> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "MZUSP" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/571a17f5-b06d-4fc8-8c74-a18ff480b304> <https://schema.org/description> "The Museum of Zoology was established in the 1890s, when several zoological collections came together to form the Museu Paulista. Since 1969, the Museum has been part of the University of São Paulo (USP). Today, the Museum of Zoology of the University of São Paulo (MZUSP) holds one of the largest zoological collections in Latin America and plays a crucial role in advancing knowledge of Brazilian and global biodiversity. With more than 10 million preserved specimens, the Museum safeguards unique records of species and ecosystems, including some that are now extinct. This heritage provides an invaluable source of data for research in evolutionary biology, paleontology, ecology, and molecular biology. This information, in turn, supports studies and initiatives in environmental monitoring, climate change, and bioprospecting—topics of major relevance today. " .
