<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/334f6c8d-5727-446c-a8c9-afcceb142c33> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/334f6c8d-5727-446c-a8c9-afcceb142c33> <https://schema.org/name> "Museo Nacional de Historia Natural" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/334f6c8d-5727-446c-a8c9-afcceb142c33> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "MNHN" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/334f6c8d-5727-446c-a8c9-afcceb142c33> <https://schema.org/description> "The Museo Nacional de Historia Natural is an institution created in 1837, it is the oldest scientific institution of Uruguay. Its main tasks are research, conservation, education and dissemination of Natural Sciences in Uruguay. The MNHN develops and maintains scientific collections that contain 400.000 specimens and serve as the primary repositories for the country. The collection is composed by Botany: It has a Herbarium of about 80,000 plants, mainly phanerograms, mostly from Uruguay. Paleontology: composed almost exclusively fossil vertebrates of the Mesozoic and Cenozoic of Uruguay. Zoology: subdivided by zoological groups, are noted for their representativeness collections spiders, fish, molluscs, amphibians, birds, mammals and reptiles. Our specimens, tissue samples and related specimen data are available to qualified members of the scientific community affiliated with academic and research institutions. We especially encourage the use of the collections in student research, whether as thesis work, independent study, or class projects. Also the research of the museum covers areas in systematics, biodiversity, conservation, natural resources, protected areas and climate change." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/334f6c8d-5727-446c-a8c9-afcceb142c33> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3328392> .
