<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/bd9f89fe-399e-4a33-95d2-138eb9bffd47> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/bd9f89fe-399e-4a33-95d2-138eb9bffd47> <https://schema.org/name> "Natural History Museum Rijeka" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/bd9f89fe-399e-4a33-95d2-138eb9bffd47> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "NHMR" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/bd9f89fe-399e-4a33-95d2-138eb9bffd47> <https://schema.org/description> "Entered Sep 2009. Specimens are stored in acid free herbarium paper and are kept in metal storage cabinets, while corresponding samples of selected taxa are conserved in 96% ethanol / glycerol (50:50) and/or silicagel dried.; The herbarium is equipped with cabinet drier.; The herbarium data is completely digitalized (database in FileMaker Pro 9), all herbarium sheets are photographed while (for the moment) web browsing is available for about half of the specimens. Internet access to all the data will be possible by the end of 2009. Primarily specimens from SE Europe (Croatia, Slovenia, Italia, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Montenegro, Serbia, Romania, Albania, Greece), Balkan peninsula, Dinaric Alps and Liburnian karst. The herbarium is one of the most comprehensive collections of the plant genera (Campanulaceae), (Plantaginaceae), (Apiaceae) and (Caryophyllaceae), and special emphasis is put on collecting specimens of polymorphic genera , , , etc." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/bd9f89fe-399e-4a33-95d2-138eb9bffd47> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(14.44445 45.329479)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

