<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dcee6ada-6960-4f64-88b2-640a2a2a2c11> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dcee6ada-6960-4f64-88b2-640a2a2a2c11> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dcee6ada-6960-4f64-88b2-640a2a2a2c11> <https://schema.org/name> "Bristol Museum & Art Gallery" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dcee6ada-6960-4f64-88b2-640a2a2a2c11> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "BRSMG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/dcee6ada-6960-4f64-88b2-640a2a2a2c11> <https://schema.org/description> "Bristol Museums' natural sciences collections were established in 1823 by the Bristol Institution for the Advancement of Science, Literature and the Arts. The earliest-collected material they incorporate dates from 1750s. An estimated 1.3 million specimens cover botany and mycology, invertebrate and vertebrate zoology, palaeontology, petrology, mineralogy. These have been collected locally and from around the world. They are complemented by a rich archive, library and geological map collection. The museum service operates across five sites and includes collections of the arts, archaeology, history and world cultures. The service's vision is: 'Using Culture as the catalyst, we play a leading role in driving a city of hope and aspiration where everyone can share in its success'. The institution holds material from across the UK and the world, as well as extra-terrestrial material. There is strong local and regional representation from Bristol and South West England. Global collections reflect the city's history as a major sea trading port, and the colonial and international connections of its donors, including prominent Bristol dynastic families, the Bristol Naturalists' Society, the University of Bristol and Bristol Zoo. There is material from all continents in the collection. Major geographic areas represented include: Africa (particularly West, Central and East); Australia; Europe; South America; North America (including significant material from the West Indies); Asia." .
