<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0a76df5c-a78c-4ab8-8e0b-74fa19e8eadc> <https://schema.org/name> "Bündner Naturmuseum, Chur" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0a76df5c-a78c-4ab8-8e0b-74fa19e8eadc> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "BNM" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0a76df5c-a78c-4ab8-8e0b-74fa19e8eadc> <https://schema.org/description> "https://www.gbif.org/grscicoll/institution/0a76df5c-a78c-4ab8-8e0b-74fa19e8eadc The Natural History Museum of the Grisons is a centre of information on natural science. Its various activities present the nature and the characteristics of the canton Grisons to the broad public. A major aspect of the activities is documenting the nature of the Grisons and its changes. The rich collections and its own research suit this purpose. Collections The collections are the core of the museum and indispensable for science. Most objects of the collections come from Grisons. The oldest collections are 200 years old. Others were built up a short time ago and have been supplemented continuously. Especially worth mentioning are the collections from the Swiss National Park and the one of mammals. Botany: The collection of vascular plants is extensive. The so-called &ldquo;Herbarium Raeticum&rdquo; covers examples of vascular plants collected in Graub&uuml;nden within the last 200 years. World-famous Josias Braun-Blanquet (1884-1980), a pioneer of plant ecology, had a herbarium of his own in his parental home in Chur. This herbarium is at the Natural History Museum of the Grisons, while his other herbariums are stored at the University of Montpellier. The oldest herbarium of the Museum was collected at the beginning of the 19th century by the B&uuml;ndner pastor Luzius Pool. Zoology: Since the foundation of the museum mammals have been continuously collected. And since 1970 small mammals (rodents, insectivores, small carnivores) have been systematically sampled. This collection is one of the most extensive in the whole area of the Alps. Various collections from the Swiss National Park as well as the collection of butterflies by Thomann and the collection of snails by Wolf are scientifically significant. Recent collections are those of wild bees and butterflies. Geology: The geological collections include many special pieces collected by well-known Bündner geologists. The rock collection of big tunnel constructions in Grisons is outstanding. On display there are fossils from the region of the Swiss National Park, the region of Piz Aela (fish saurian) and relicts of fish saurian from the Ducan area near Davos. Fragments of cave bear bones (between 50'000 and 100'000 years old) were found in the caves of Sulzfluh near St. Antönien. Switzerland, especially canton of Graubünden." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0a76df5c-a78c-4ab8-8e0b-74fa19e8eadc> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1020808> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0a76df5c-a78c-4ab8-8e0b-74fa19e8eadc> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(9.53451 46.853619)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0b129262-5b48-43f6-b53f-615980000297> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0b129262-5b48-43f6-b53f-615980000297> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0b129262-5b48-43f6-b53f-615980000297> <https://schema.org/name> "Shetland Museum and Archives" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0b129262-5b48-43f6-b53f-615980000297> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "SMAA" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0b129262-5b48-43f6-b53f-615980000297> <https://schema.org/description> "Shetland Museum is the local authority museum for Shetland. It is managed by Shetland Amenity Trust via a SLA with the Shetland Islands Council. The collection covers the cultural and natural heritage of the Shetland archipelago, including inshore waters. Shetland Museum holds two Recognised Collections in Archaeology and Textiles. Other strong areas of the collection are marine archaeology, early crofting material, an herbarium collected by Walter Scott, lichen collections by Kery Dalby, and a geological collection by Derek Flinn. Shetland is a UNESCO Global Geopark. Shetland Museum has no subject specialists for its natural history collections. Geographical scope of the collection is the Shetland archipelago. Includes objects brought to Shetland by sailors, whalers (Greenland, South Georgia), trade items by local merchant sailors and items salvaged from shipwrecks, some of which originated in the East Indies, Asia, and Europe." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/0b129262-5b48-43f6-b53f-615980000297> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/01gw2fq77> .
