<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3b896e03-901d-4cc7-ac2b-61e0469521b0> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3b896e03-901d-4cc7-ac2b-61e0469521b0> <https://schema.org/name> "Meise Botanic Garden" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3b896e03-901d-4cc7-ac2b-61e0469521b0> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "BR" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3b896e03-901d-4cc7-ac2b-61e0469521b0> <https://schema.org/description> "The collections of Meise Botanic Garden constitute an internationally significant scientific resource for botanical research, biodiversity conservation, and ecosystem studies. They include extensive living collections, a seed bank preserving wild plant genetic diversity, nearly four million herbarium specimens, and specialised library and archival holdings documenting botanical knowledge from the 15th century to the present. These collections support taxonomic, phylogenetic, ecological, and conservation research, with particular expertise in economically important groups such as wild coffee, banana, bean, and edible mushroom species. By integrating ex situ conservation, digitisation, and long-term biodiversity monitoring, the Garden contributes to global efforts addressing species loss, climate change, and future food security. Together, the collections provide an essential scientific infrastructure for advancing plant systematics, sustainable ecosystem management, and the preservation of botanical heritage for future generations." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/3b896e03-901d-4cc7-ac2b-61e0469521b0> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q3052500> .
