<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f0898dfe-aae0-456c-af84-cbc91bf06ec7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f0898dfe-aae0-456c-af84-cbc91bf06ec7> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f0898dfe-aae0-456c-af84-cbc91bf06ec7> <https://schema.org/name> "Reading Museum" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f0898dfe-aae0-456c-af84-cbc91bf06ec7> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "REDMG" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f0898dfe-aae0-456c-af84-cbc91bf06ec7> <https://schema.org/description> "Reading Museum began in 1883 with a bequest from local collector Horatio Bland, who left the Borough his museum of fascinating objects from around the world. The focus is now on collecting objects linked with Reading, its people and environment. The natural history collections consist of over 200,000 specimens reflecting both the biodiversity of the Reading area and the interests of local collectors. Three-quarters of the collection is entomological with good representation of the British fauna, particularly Lepidoptera, and some material from Europe, Africa and India. shells, corals, rocks, minerals and fossils. The museum is located in the town centre and houses the bulk of the entomology collection as well as a gallery displaying local fossils, plants and animals, but most of the reserve collections are at our offsite store. Mostly southern England, also elsewhere in the UK and Europe (all), Africa and Asia (Lepidoptera, some botany and geology, few vertebrates), Pacific (shells). Individual items from other regions e.g. tektites from Australia" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/f0898dfe-aae0-456c-af84-cbc91bf06ec7> <https://schema.org/sameAs> <https://ror.org/00akavp61> .
