<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26767fe2-9f84-4824-961f-c9a6940effab> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26767fe2-9f84-4824-961f-c9a6940effab> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Museum> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26767fe2-9f84-4824-961f-c9a6940effab> <https://schema.org/name> "Komarov Botanical Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26767fe2-9f84-4824-961f-c9a6940effab> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "BIN RAS" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26767fe2-9f84-4824-961f-c9a6940effab> <https://schema.org/description> "Komarov Botanical Institute of the Russian Academy of Sciences is one of Russia's oldest scientific institutions, established in 1714 by Emperor Peter the Great as a Pharmaceutical Garden (\"Aptekarsky ogorod\"). While initially created to grow medicinal plants for the army, it quickly evolved into a scientific research center. The institute as it exists today was formed in 1931 through the merger of two significant botanical institutions: the Botanical Garden (formerly the Imperial Botanical Garden) and the Botanical Museum of the Imperial Academy of Sciences. In 1940, it was named after the prominent Russian botanist Vladimir Komarov. The institute currently comprises 20 scientific departments conducting research across virtually all major areas of modern botany and mycology. Its primary activities include: - Taxonomy, phylogeny, evolution and ecology of plants and fungi - Study of modern and fossil flora of Russia and foreign countries - Research on structure, dynamics and ecology of plant communities - Structural and functional organization of plants and fungi - Plant introduction and botanical resource science - Plant protection - Preservation and expansion of collection holdings - Promotion of botanical knowledge The institute maintains significant scientific collections that serve as the foundation for botanical research in Russia and internationally." .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26767fe2-9f84-4824-961f-c9a6940effab> <http://www.wikidata.org/prop/direct/P625> "POINT(30.323166 59.971099)"^^<http://www.opengis.net/ont/geosparql#wktLiteral> .

<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26944ca1-0778-4563-aad9-56d45f6028c1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/Organization> .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26944ca1-0778-4563-aad9-56d45f6028c1> <https://schema.org/name> "Senckenberg Gesellschaft für Naturforschung: Research Station for Quaternary Palaeontology" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26944ca1-0778-4563-aad9-56d45f6028c1> <https://schema.org/alternateName> "IQW" .
<https://scientific-collections.gbif.org/institution/26944ca1-0778-4563-aad9-56d45f6028c1> <https://schema.org/description> "Entered Mar 2009. This station is part of a decentralized institution (Senckenberg Research Institutes and Natural History Museums) which also comprises botanical research departments and herbaria in Görlitz (GLM) and Frankfurt/Main (FR). Flora of Beringia (Yakutia [NE-Siberia], Alaska) and Europe as reference for the identification of fossil plant parts, vascular plants, special focus on arctic, boreal and steppe plants" .
